# 20. JUnit Dependency Management with BOM

A more flexible JUnit configuration is:

```gradle
dependencies {

    testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')

    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'

    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'

    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'
}
```

This configuration can work because the JUnit BOM manages compatible versions of the JUnit modules.

What is the BOM?

BOM means:

```text
Bill of Materials
```

The JUnit BOM:

```gradle
testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')
```

provides version information for related JUnit dependencies.

Therefore, you can write:

```gradle
testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'
```

without explicitly specifying its version.

The version is obtained from the BOM.

# 21. Why junit-platform-launcher Is Added

This dependency:

```gradle
testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'
```

provides the JUnit Platform Launcher at test runtime.

The important distinction is:

```text
testImplementation
        ↓
Required to compile test code

testRuntimeOnly
        ↓
Required when tests are executed
```

So:

```gradle
testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'
```

is used for compiling the test code, while:

```gradle
testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'
```

makes the launcher available at test runtime.

# 22. Attempt to Force JUnit Platform Version

Another approach is:

```gradle
configurations.all {
    resolutionStrategy.eachDependency { dependency ->
        if (dependency.requested.group == 'org.junit.platform') {
            dependency.useVersion '1.12.2'
        }
    }
}
```

This approach is different from using a BOM.

It tells Gradle:

For dependencies whose group is:

```text
org.junit.platform
```

force the requested version to:

```text
1.12.2
```

Conceptually:

```text
Dependency resolution
        │
        ▼
Check each dependency
        │
        ▼
Is group == org.junit.platform?
        │
       Yes
        │
        ▼
Use version 1.12.2
```

However, this is not equivalent to the JUnit BOM.

The BOM manages a coordinated set of JUnit versions, while `resolutionStrategy` modifies dependency resolution based on a rule.

Therefore, if the BOM-based configuration works while the `resolutionStrategy` configuration does not, the BOM approach is generally preferable for managing JUnit's related module versions.

# 23. BOM vs resolutionStrategy

BOM approach:

```gradle
dependencies {
    testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')

    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'

    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'
}
```

Conceptually:

```text
JUnit BOM
   │
   ├── JUnit Jupiter
   ├── JUnit Platform
   └── Other compatible JUnit modules
```

The BOM is designed to keep related JUnit components compatible.

Resolution strategy approach:

```gradle
configurations.all {
    resolutionStrategy.eachDependency { dependency ->
        if (dependency.requested.group == 'org.junit.platform') {
            dependency.useVersion '1.12.2'
        }
    }
}
```

This is a general Gradle dependency-resolution mechanism.

It does not provide the same coordinated dependency management that the JUnit BOM provides.

# 24. Recommended JUnit Configuration

For the example project, a clean configuration is:

```gradle
plugins {
    id 'java'
}

group = 'com.example'
version = '1.0.0'

repositories {
    mavenCentral()
}

dependencies {

    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'

    testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')
    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'

    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'
}

test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}
```

The main concepts are:

```text
Gradle Wrapper
    ↓
Controls Gradle version

settings.gradle
    ↓
Defines project structure/name

build.gradle
    ↓
Defines how the project is built

plugins
    ↓
Adds Gradle capabilities

repositories
    ↓
Defines where dependencies are found

dependencies
    ↓
Defines libraries required by the project

BOM
    ↓
Manages compatible dependency versions

testImplementation
    ↓
Dependencies required to compile tests

testRuntimeOnly
    ↓
Dependencies required when tests execute

useJUnitPlatform()
    ↓
Uses JUnit Platform for test execution

gradlew build
    ↓
Compiles + tests + packages the application
```

# 25. Gradle Dependency Configurations

## 25.1 implementation

Dependency required by your application:

```gradle
dependencies {
    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'
}
```

Used by:

```text
src/main/java
```

Example:

```text
StringUtils.isBlank(name);
```

## 25.2 testImplementation

Dependency required only for tests:

```gradle
testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter:5.12.2'
```

Available to:

```text
src/test/java
```

but not your main application.

## 25.3 runtimeOnly

Required when the application runs, but not needed to compile your Java source.

Example with PostgreSQL:

```gradle
dependencies {
    implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
    runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'
}
```

Conceptually:

```text
Compile:
    JDBC API
    PostgreSQL driver is not needed directly by the application code

Runtime:
    JDBC API
    PostgreSQL driver
```

This is very common for database drivers.

## 25.4 testRuntimeOnly

Required only when tests run.

Example:

```gradle
testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'
```

Compile tests:

```text
JUnit API
```

Run tests:

```text
JUnit API
JUnit Engine
JUnit Launcher
```

## 25.5 compileOnly

Needed to compile your code but should not be included at runtime.

Example:

```gradle
compileOnly 'org.projectlombok:lombok:1.18.38'
```

Common example:

```text
Lombok
   ↓
needed during compilation
   ↓
not needed in application runtime
```

## 25.6 annotationProcessor

For annotation processors such as Lombok:

```gradle
dependencies {
    compileOnly 'org.projectlombok:lombok:1.18.38'
    annotationProcessor 'org.projectlombok:lombok:1.18.38'
}
```

The annotation processor generates/modifies code during compilation.

## 25.7 testCompileOnly

Similar to `compileOnly`, but only for test compilation:

```gradle
testCompileOnly 'some:test-library:1.0'
```

Not needed very often in basic projects.

## 25.8 testAnnotationProcessor

Annotation processor used only for tests:

```gradle
testAnnotationProcessor 'some:test-processor:1.0'
```

Again, less commonly needed.

# 26. Most Important Dependency Configurations

For normal Java/Spring projects, remember these first:

```text
Configuration             Purpose

implementation             Application compile + runtime

runtimeOnly                Application runtime only

compileOnly                Compile only

annotationProcessor        Compile-time code generation

testImplementation         Test compile + test runtime

testRuntimeOnly            Test runtime only

testCompileOnly            Test compile only

testAnnotationProcessor    Test annotation processing
```

# 27. A Practical Example

```gradle
dependencies {

    // Application
    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'

    // Database driver
    runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql:42.7.7'

    // Compile-time
    compileOnly 'org.projectlombok:lombok:1.18.38'
    annotationProcessor 'org.projectlombok:lombok:1.18.38'

    // Tests
    testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')
    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'

    // Test runtime
    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'
}
```

# 28. Dependency Scopes

Think of it as:

```text
                     Dependency
                         │
          ┌──────────────┼──────────────┐
          ↓              ↓              ↓
       Compile         Runtime         Test
          │              │              │
 implementation     runtimeOnly   testImplementation
 compileOnly                       testRuntimeOnly
 annotationProcessor
```

