# 30. Maven vs Gradle Dependency Management

In Maven, Spring Boot commonly has:

```xml
<parent>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
    <version>3.4.2</version>
</parent>
```

The parent provides things such as:

```text
Dependency versions
Plugin versions/configuration
Default Maven configuration
```

Gradle does not use inheritance through a `<parent>` POM.

Instead, these responsibilities are split among plugins and dependency management.

For Spring Boot:

```gradle
plugins {
    id 'java'
    id 'org.springframework.boot' version '3.4.2'
}
```

The Spring Boot plugin gives you Spring Boot build functionality and dependency management support.

Then:

```gradle
dependencies {
    implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
    runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'
}
```

You don't need to specify the versions because Spring Boot manages compatible dependency versions.

# 31. Maven vs Gradle

MAVEN

```xml
<parent>
    spring-boot-starter-parent
        ↓
    dependency versions
    plugin configuration
    defaults
</parent>
```

GRADLE

```gradle
plugins {
    id 'org.springframework.boot' version '3.4.2'
}
```

```text
        ↓
Spring Boot Gradle plugin
        ↓
Spring Boot dependency management
        ↓
implementation / runtimeOnly dependencies
```

So if you're asking:

```text
Where do I specify the equivalent of Spring Boot's parent version?
```

In Gradle, normally here:

```gradle
id 'org.springframework.boot' version '3.4.2'
```

There is no separate parent block like Maven.

# 32. Spring Boot JDBC and PostgreSQL

Spring Boot JDBC:

```gradle
implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
```

PostgreSQL driver:

```gradle
runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'
```

Does starter-jdbc contain PostgreSQL?

No.

`spring-boot-starter-jdbc` does not contain the PostgreSQL JDBC driver.

These are two different things:

```gradle
implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'
```

# 33. What spring-boot-starter-jdbc Contains

Conceptually:

```text
spring-boot-starter-jdbc
        │
        ├── Spring JDBC
        ├── Spring TX
        ├── HikariCP
        └── Spring Boot JDBC support
```

It provides the framework support for connecting to databases, but it does not know which database you are using.

# 34. PostgreSQL Driver

You separately add:

```gradle
runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'
```

This provides the actual PostgreSQL JDBC driver.

The flow is:

```text
Your application
       │
       ▼
Spring JDBC
       │
       ▼
JDBC API
       │
       ▼
PostgreSQL JDBC Driver
       │
       ▼
PostgreSQL Database
```

# 35. Why runtimeOnly for the Database Driver?

Your Java code generally uses JDBC/Spring JDBC APIs:

```java
JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;
```

It doesn't directly need PostgreSQL-specific classes to compile.

The PostgreSQL driver is needed when the application actually connects to PostgreSQL.

Therefore:

```gradle
implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'
```

is the normal setup.

# 36. Compare H2 and PostgreSQL

If you use H2:

```gradle
implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
runtimeOnly 'com.h2database:h2'
```

For PostgreSQL:

```gradle
implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'
```

For MySQL:

```gradle
implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
runtimeOnly 'com.mysql:mysql-connector-j'
```

The JDBC starter is database-independent; the database driver is selected separately.

